# Spec authoring

**Rule Rev.4** — 2026-09-13 (common)

Latest copy lives in **DialKey** `.cursor/rules`. The Japanese essay
`project_standards/app_spec_rules.md` is older — do not use it to override
DialKey. If they disagree, follow DialKey and fix the essay.

Write the spec **before** coding. Keep it a living document.
Public spec text is **English** (`docs-language.mdc`).
Start a new project from the **minimum set**; add full-set items as
complexity grows (UI, persistence, external APIs).

## Minimum set (small personal tools)

1. Feature list
2. Screen-flow diagram (if there is a UI)
3. Table / persistence definitions (if data is stored)

## Full set — add what the project actually needs

**Upstream:** purpose, users, glossary, constraints, **language/stack choice
with reasons**, architecture pattern when extension is expected.

**Functions:** features with priority, use cases, process flows, screen
design, state machines.

**Data:** ER / table definitions, data flow.

**Interfaces:** API and external-system contracts.

**Non-functional:** performance, security, operations, error handling.

**Other:** directory / architecture diagram, test spec, revision history.

Need/skip of full-set items is judged per project (UI? persistence? external
APIs?). When a repeatable rule appears, add it here and bump this Rev.

## Stack choice (when others may run the binary)

Prefer a single artifact, static types for strangers, licences that fit the
project licence, and memory-safe languages in compliance-sensitive domains.
Python is fine for private/small tools; it is a weaker default for unknown
machines. For an OSS **server / core** app that others may run, treat a
typed language that packs to a single artifact (**Rust**) as
the default candidate. Do not force a three-layer core/overlay/tenant split
on a tool with no expected variants.

## Architecture when variants will grow

If the unit of difference is already clear (locale, tenant, customer):

1. **Core** — no named-target branches; interprets declarative config only
2. **Overlay** — per-target packages; do not edit core
3. **Tenant** — further overrides of overlay data only

Add a backward-compatible core extension only when the existing DSL cannot
express the case.

## Project docs

Keep project documents in one place (e.g. `docs/` or `/areas/`) with a
revision history. Do not leave a spec as a one-shot draft.
